+# Decision: Committed-change delivery transport
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard keeps one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; the server assigns each committed edit one event revision. Something must carry committed revisions from the server to the browsers displaying a board, and that choice shapes three coupled components: how revisions are stored and read (change store), how they reach browsers (delivery), and how a browser recovers a lost connection (browser sync).
+
+The constraints are already fixed. Connected browsers must show committed changes within 15 seconds under normal connectivity, and subsecond updates are useful but not required. A browser disconnected for up to 20 minutes must still recover the latest board. A busy market runs 10-25 editors, bursts to 20 edits per second around opening, and up to 800 simultaneous browsers. Seven days of revisions are retained. Both self-hosted and managed infrastructure are permitted.
+
+The transport is not a detail of one component: choosing it allocates work across all three—what the store must retain, what delivery must own, and how much recovery logic the browser must carry. Getting it wrong means re-cutting those boundaries, so the trade-off is recorded here in full rather than as a single rationale line.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+- **Update latency** (moderate) — how quickly a committed revision becomes visible to connected browsers. The requirement is 15 seconds; lower latency is a quality gain, not a compliance gate.
+- **Recurring cost** (major) — the ongoing spend to deliver revisions, including any per-connection or per-message fees.
+- **Vendor dependence** (major) — how much the board's delivery relies on an external provider's availability, terms, and interface, and how costly it is to leave.
+- **Operational burden** (major) — the connection, session, and failure handling the project must run and staff.
+- **Failure visibility** (moderate) — whether a delivery failure is distinguishable from an unchanged board. Derived from the tenet *Connectivity failures must be visible, not mistaken for an unchanged board.*
+- **Repeated-read load** (minor) — delivery work spent reading a board that has not changed.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+Each browser re-requests the board every five seconds over HTTPS. The server answers each request from a cursor read into the revision history. Delivery stays request/response: no connection is kept alive between requests.
+
+**Analysis**
+
+- Update latency: **partial** — a change appears on the next poll, so up to about five seconds of lag.
+- Recurring cost: **strong** — no provider fee; ordinary HTTPS serving.
+- Vendor dependence: **strong** — none.
+- Operational burden: **strong** — stateless; there are no sessions to manage or reconnect. The simplest option to operate.
+- Failure visibility: **weak** — a browser that cannot reach the server keeps showing its last board. Unless the client explicitly distinguishes a failed fetch from an unchanged read, stale and healthy look alike.
+- Repeated-read load: **weak** — every browser re-reads every five seconds even when nothing changed, about 160 reads per second at 800 browsers.
+
+**Summary** — cheapest to run and simplest to operate, at the cost of lag, repeated reads, and a failure mode that resembles an unchanged board.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+The server keeps a persistent WebSocket connection per browser. On commit, delivery publishes the revision to every open session, and browsers apply revisions as they arrive. Reconnect and catch-up are the browser's responsibility: after a dropped connection it re-establishes and replays from its last-known revision.
+
+**Analysis**
+
+- Update latency: **strong** — a committed revision reaches connected browsers as soon as delivery publishes it.
+- Recurring cost: **strong** — no provider fee; the cost is running and scaling the socket tier.
+- Vendor dependence: **strong** — none.
+- Operational burden: **weak** — the project owns connection capacity, session lifecycle, and reconnect behavior, and a failure in the socket tier touches every connected browser.
+- Failure visibility: **strong** — a dropped connection is an explicit state the browser can surface, distinct from an unchanged board.
+- Repeated-read load: **strong** — the server pushes only committed revisions; no idle re-reads.
+
+**Summary** — fast push and full control, paid for in connection and reconnect operations the project must own.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+A third-party realtime provider holds the client connections. The server publishes each committed revision to the provider, which fans it out to subscribed browsers, and the browser follows the provider's subscription and reconnect model.
+
+**Analysis**
+
+- Update latency: **strong** — push as soon as the provider receives the revision.
+- Recurring cost: **weak** — a recurring provider bill that scales with connections and messages.
+- Vendor dependence: **weak** — delivery depends on the provider's availability, terms, and interface; leaving requires re-implementing the delivery path.
+- Operational burden: **strong** — the provider absorbs connection capacity and reconnect handling.
+- Failure visibility: **partial** — providers expose connection state, but what the board can show is bounded by the provider's subscription model.
+- Repeated-read load: **strong** — push; no idle re-reads.
+
+**Summary** — least connection operations and fast updates, at recurring cost and provider coupling.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Self-hosted WebSockets.** It matches hosted realtime on latency and read efficiency and matches polling on cost and vendor independence; it fails only on operational burden, which is the single cost the project has chosen to accept. The decision turns on the tenet *Connectivity failures must be visible, not mistaken for an unchanged board*: a socket that drops is an explicit, surfaceable state, whereas polling's default behavior shows a stale board that reads as unchanged. It is taken deliberately against operational burden—owning connection capacity and reconnect behavior is the price of fast updates without tying the board to a realtime vendor.
+
+Implications committed by this choice:
+
+- **Change store** retains replayable revisions and publishes committed revisions reliably to delivery.
+- **Delivery** owns WebSocket session management and publication of committed revisions to connected sessions.
+- **Browser sync** owns subscription, reconnect, and catch-up from the last-known revision, meeting the 20-minute recovery goal.
+- Forecloses a cursor-query polling delivery path and provider publication reconciliation with provider credentials.
