 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Committed changes over self-hosted WebSockets.** Delivery keeps fast updates without a realtime vendor bill, at the cost of owning connection capacity and reconnect behavior. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected for its repeated reads and seconds of lag; a hosted realtime service was rejected for its recurring fees and provider coupling. All three meet the freshness goal, so the deciding trade-off is vendor independence against operational burden. See `docs/decisions/change-delivery-transport.md`.
